# meta-deploy.list: the queue meta-deploy.bash runs, in this order.
#
# STANDING RULE (CLAUDE/AgentNotes.md): this list is exactly what needs running NOW. Add a
# run when it is pending, remove it once it has run and is done, leave out what cannot
# usefully run yet, then ask the owner to run meta-deploy.bash. Every step a run needs lives
# in the plan's own scripts, so this is the only command the owner is handed. The order is by
# dependency. A plan with none of triage.bash, deploy.bash and acceptance.bash has nothing
# for the script to do. A triage-only plan is run once, read-only. Words after the folder
# name are passed to that plan's triage.bash, both runs.
#
# One entry per line, four kinds:
#   <plan folder>                  its triage, deploy and acceptance scripts, in order
#   playbooks/<path>.yml           a change no plan owns; run as one unit through its own
#                                  shebang, which goes through run.bash, exactly as
#                                  ./playbooks/imports/<play>.yml does by hand
#   <plan folder>/<script>.bash    one plan script, for a step none of the three fixed names
#                                  fits; run as one unit, like a playbook entry, except
#                                  that a script named *acceptance*.bash may exit 2 for
#                                  COULD NOT ESTABLISH, as an acceptance.bash may
#   changed-plays                  every play that has run here and has to run again, as
#                                  one unit: the checkout's ./run.bash --changed --yes, in
#                                  playbook-main.yml order, stopping at the first failure
#
# changed-plays runs WHERE IT STANDS in the list, like every other entry, and decides which
# plays it runs when it starts, so the entries above it have already run (a play an entry
# above ran is no longer changed, and is not run twice). Put it above the plan entries whose
# deploy or acceptance expects the changed plays to have run, which is the usual case, and
# below an entry it must follow. It replaces a hand-copied list of stale plays: name a
# playbooks/ entry only for a play that must run at a fixed point between two plan steps.
# Its description says which changed plays the agent expects it to pick up and why.
#
# A line starting with # is a comment and is the description of the entry below it: it is how
# the owner learns what a run will do, so write it. Blank lines are ignored. Any other line
# that is not an entry is a fatal error naming its line number.

# The plays changed since they last ran here. Expected: play-vm-test-lab.yml (Plan 00192
# Task 3.10, the review follow-ups): vmtest's retention.log names the files prune-runs
# --yes removes and records a removal that failed; the nightly sweep says a kept build on
# stderr instead of logging it every night; the free-space figure counts statfs fragments.
# The play rebuilds and reinstalls vmtest; no guest is booted, nothing is removed and nothing
# is asked. The host agent then checks, read-only, that `vmtest prune-runs` (the dry run) still
# lists its plan and that the next vmtest-nightly timer run's journal shows no new kept build
# line in retention.log; it does not run vmtest nightly or prune-runs --yes by hand.
# Also expected, for the project_duration_overflow fixes (Plan 00192 criterion 3):
# play-vm-test-lab.yml's vmtest refuses VMTEST_MEMBER_SSH_TIMEOUT over 86400 and prunes by
# calendar days; play-claude-yolo.yml rebuilds ccy-sessions (reboot and notify take at most
# 10080 minutes, verify-restore --wait at most 86400 seconds, its timing knobs at most a day);
# play-speech-to-text.yml rebuilds wsi (a stop grace over the setting's 30 seconds is refused).
# Rebuilds only: nothing is rebooted, recorded or pruned, and nothing is asked.
changed-plays
# Plan 00228 Task 6.7: the consumer VM scenario installs speech-to-text. deploy.bash runs
# play-vm-test-lab.yml (asks for sudo once): it rebuilds vmtest, whose attendant now answers
# the consumer's optional-app menu with the scenario's picks, and deploys the manifest
# (desktop-consumer-install picks play-speech-to-text.yml) and its checker (20 checks: the
# panel extension active, Whisper set up, post-processing-offered false). acceptance.bash
# then runs desktop-consumer-install, desktop-fresh-install and server-fast-provision, each on
# a throwaway guest at the pushed HEAD (it reports COULD NOT ESTABLISH if HEAD is not pushed);
# the consumer run is long, as speech-to-text downloads its models. Nothing on the host
# changes beyond the lab.
00228-consumer-install-without-github
# Plan 00235, URGENT: the store repair, then the prune finished. The first prune left three
# layers c/storage could not delete (files owned by uids of the old subuid range), and since
# then every podman image operation fails: ccy launches and builds, vmtest, the prune timer.
# 1. triage.bash --du (read-only, no sudo): the before figures; with the store broken its
#    podman legs fail, which is recorded and does not stop the run.
# 2. deploy.bash (Tasks 4.5-4.6, asks for sudo once): play-podman.yml first REMOVES, AS ROOT,
#    each leftover c/storage temporary directory (overlay/tempdirs/temp-dir-N and its lock-N)
#    in the user's rootless store, naming each and its size in the play output; it stops
#    instead, removing nothing, if a podman or buildah of the user is removing or building or a
#    lock there is held. That makes podman work again before the play asks it anything, so the
#    migrate step cannot stop the running containers on a failed probe. It then rebuilds
#    container-images and starts the prune once more (no prune has succeeded yet): dangling
#    images no container uses (over a day old), unused claude-yolo project images over 30 days,
#    buildah leftovers, each logged to ~/.local/state/fedora-desktop/container-images.log; no
#    volume, no image in use. If another layer holds old-range files the prune now stops at it
#    and exits 1, the play repairs the store again as root and then fails: the store is left
#    working, and the failure is Q6 of the plan (old-range files still in the store), the
#    owner's decision. Running containers are not stopped. It may take some minutes.
#    Before the timer, a root probe searches the store for a file owned outside the user's uid
#    and current subuid range. If it finds one, the play does NOT run the prune, STOPS and
#    DISABLES container-images-prune.timer, and FAILS ON PURPOSE at its end with "the podman
#    store holds files owned outside your subuid range (old range); the daily prune is held off
#    until they are re-owned (Plan 00235 Q6)". That failure is expected: the store is repaired
#    and podman works, only the daily prune waits for Q6.
# 3. triage.bash --du again: the after figures.
# 4. acceptance.bash (read-only; skipped if the deploy failed): the timer is enabled with a
#    next run, the prune succeeded and logged, no unused dangling image over a day old is
#    left, every container still has its image, claude-yolo:base and :latest exist.
00235-podman-images-cleaned-up-automatically --du
